{
  "schema_version": 1,
  "list_id": "claude-opus-5-5",
  "run": 3,
  "simulation_id": "sim-04",
  "member_id": "johansson-goran",
  "model": "gpt-5.6-terra",
  "reasoning_effort": "high",
  "prompt": "prompts/physics_committee_terra_round2_v1.md",
  "preferred_configuration": {
    "prize_parts": [
      {
        "ballot_id": "B038",
        "laureates": [
          "Hidetoshi Katori",
          "Jun Ye"
        ],
        "citation": "for the invention and development of optical lattice clocks"
      }
    ]
  },
  "alternatives": [
    "B030",
    "B008"
  ],
  "statement": {
    "case_for": "My configuration is unchanged. Optical lattice clocks constitute a completed conceptual and experimental advance: magic-wavelength confinement made it possible to exploit many neutral atoms while controlling the light shift that would otherwise compromise clock precision. Katori and Ye provide a compact, complementary attribution to the originating idea and defining experimental development. The decisive maturity criterion is satisfied by independent reproducibility: this is an established new measurement regime, not a promising single laboratory apparatus. A Prize need not await every later application when the underlying physical method has demonstrably transformed what can be measured.",
    "case_against": "The citation must remain limited to the lattice-clock breakthrough and not absorb the wider history of optical-frequency standards or claim all subsequent metrological applications. There is also a reasonable philosophical objection that the most singular fundamental discoveries uniquely enabled by these clocks may still lie ahead. That reservation is real, but it does not outweigh the unusually coherent union of physical principle, realization, robustness, and exact attribution.",
    "responses": [
      {
        "member_id": "mehlig-bernhard",
        "point": "Mehlig argues that defect-mediated two-dimensional melting offers an already settled explanatory principle, whereas lattice clocks' prize-defining fundamental consequence is still developing.",
        "response": "I agree that B007 is profound mature physics and that a Prize should not reward technological promise alone. But B038 is not being proposed for promise: the magic-wavelength principle itself removed a concrete physical limitation and has been repeatedly realized as a robust precision-measurement method. A new, reproducible regime of observation can be prize-worthy without waiting for a later discovery made using it. The qualification on B007's correspondence to nonideal two-dimensional systems also makes its empirical scope less clean than the focused B038 citation."
      },
      {
        "member_id": "eriksson-olle",
        "point": "Eriksson favors the quantum spin Hall effect because it combines a precise theoretical prediction with a landmark experimental realization of a new topological state.",
        "response": "The theory-to-experiment chain in B022 is exceptionally attractive, and it remains a serious comparison case. I give B038 precedence because its central physical advance and two-person credit can be stated more narrowly: Katori's lattice-clock concept and Ye's decisive development. B022 still requires a difficult boundary from later topological phases and confirmation that Molenkamp alone adequately represents the experimental realization."
      },
      {
        "member_id": "pearce-mark",
        "point": "Pearce argues that IceCube opened a new astronomical messenger, while recognizing the severe problem of representing a large collaboration by Francis Halzen alone.",
        "response": "I share the excitement over high-energy neutrino astronomy and accept that the secure diffuse flux is a major achievement. The attribution obstacle is nevertheless central, not incidental: the supplied record does not establish an individual contribution sufficiently decisive to compress an intrinsically collaborative discovery to one name. B038 has comparable experimental maturity while retaining a far more defensible allocation of original credit."
      }
    ],
    "uncertainties": [
      "Whether a final historical review can define the lattice-clock achievement narrowly enough to separate it from the broader development of optical frequency standards.",
      "Whether the committee judges the established and independently reproduced measurement regime sufficient without awaiting a uniquely decisive fundamental-physics discovery enabled by optical lattice clocks."
    ],
    "conflict_note": "No direct connection to Hidetoshi Katori or Jun Ye is apparent from the supplied profile. My WACQT role and longstanding superconducting-circuit relationships would warrant recusal consideration if B029 became an active configuration; it is not my preferred configuration or an alternative here."
  }
}
